p.src:
For two galaxies $A$ and $B$, $\begin{aligned}
d_{AB}(t)&=a(t)\,\Delta\chi_{AB},\\
\Delta\chi_{AB}
&=\sqrt{(x_A-x_B)^2+(y_A-y_B)^2+(z_A-z_B)^2}.
\end{aligned}$ If they differ only in the $x$-coordinate, this reduces to $d_{AB}(t)=a(t)\,\Delta x_{AB}.$ The function $a(t)$ is the scale factor. Its absolute normalization is conventional: multiplying all comoving coordinates by a constant and dividing $a$ by the same constant changes no physical distance. Ratios such as $a(t_2)/a(t_1)$, and derivatives divided by $a$, carry the physical information.

